\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Consider the instance with $N=\{1,2\}$, $M=\{g_1,g_2,\dots,g_{2c+1}\}$, and $L=[2c+1]$.
For every agent $i\in N$, item $g_j\in M$, and dimension $k\in L$, define
$v_{ijk}$ to be $1$ if $j=k$ and $0$ otherwise.
% \begin{align}
% v_{ijk}=\begin{cases}
% 1 & \text{if }j=k,\\
% 0 & \text{if }j\ne k.
% \end{cases}
% \end{align}
This instance is both binary and identical.

Now, suppose toward a contradiction that there exists a strong sEF$c$ allocation $\bA=(A_1,A_2)$. Without loss of generality, assume that $|A_1|\le |A_2|$.
Since $|A_1|+|A_2|=|M|=2c+1$, it follows that $|A_1|\le c$ and $|A_2|\ge c+1$. 

