\subsection{Classes are perfect} \label{ss:perf}

We now show that the classes $\bE_k(n)$ are perfect, which is condition (ii) in Proposition~\ref{prop:stair}.
We use the following result, which is explained for example in \cite[Prop 1.2.12]{ball}. 
Recall also the definition of {\bf Cremona move} from Definition~\ref{def:Cremona}. 

\begin{lemma}
    An ordered integral class $\bE:=dL-\sum_{i=1}^N n_iE_i$ in $H_2(\CP^2 \# N\oCP^2)$ represents an exceptional divisor if and only if it may be reduced to $E_1$ by a repeated application of Cremona moves. 
\end{lemma}

It is convenient for our purposes here to keep track of  the initial order of the entries in the tuple $\bE_k(n)$. Therefore
we consider the transformation $c_{i,j,k}$ given by
\begin{align} c_{i,j,k}(dL-\sum_{i=1} n_iE_i)&=\widetilde{d}L-\sum_{i=1} \widetilde{n}_1E_i, \quad \text{with }  \begin{cases} \widetilde{d}= d+\delta_{ijk} \\ 
\widetilde{n}_\ell =n_\ell+\delta_{ijk} & \text{if $\ell=i,j,k$}\\
\widetilde{n}_\ell=n_\ell & \text{if $\ell \neq i,j,k$}
\end{cases}  \\ \notag
&\mbox{ where } \ \delta_{ijk}=d-n_i-n_j-n_k.
\end{align}
Further, we call 
 $\delta_{ijk}$  the \textbf{defect}. 
 Below, we write the Cremona transformation in coordinates as $$
 c_{i,j,k}(d;n_1,\hdots,n_N)=(\widetilde{d},\widetilde{n}_1,\hdots,\widetilde{n}_N),
 $$
  and the reordering operation reorders any of the $n_\ell.$

 We say two vectors are {\bf Cremona equivalent} if one can be obtained from the other via a series of Cremona moves. As Cremona moves are reversible, to verify some $\bE$ is exceptional, we can show that $\bE$ is Cremona equivalent to some other exceptional $\bE'$.  

 In this section, we will show that for each $n \geq 0$ and $k \geq 2$, the class $\bE_k(n)$ is Cremona equivalent to a particular class that has been previously shown to reduce to $(0;-1).$ Hence, we can conclude $\bE_k(n)$ is perfect. 


We first establish some properties relevant to $W(p_k(n),q_k(n)).$


The paper \cite{BHM} studied a related set of classes 
\[\bB_k(n):=(\ov{d}_k(n);\ov{m}_k(n);W(\ov{p}_k(n),\ov{q}_k(n))) \]
in the  one point blowup of $\C P^2$.  These classes are defined recursively from
the initial seeds $p_0/q_0 = (2n+6)/1, p_1/q_1 = (2n+7)/(2n+4)$ with the recursion variable $t = 2n+5$.
In \cite[Theorem 56]{BHM}, it was shown\footnote{The paper \cite{BHM} uses the notation $\Ss^U_{\ell,n}$ to refer to the family of classes $\bB_k(n)$.} that for $ k \geq 2$, the continued fractions of the centers of $\bB_k(n)$ are 
\begin{equation} \label{eq:CFBnk}
\ov{p}_k(n)/\ov{q}_k(n)=\begin{cases}
  [\{2n+7,2n+3\}^{\lfloor(k-2)/2\rfloor},2n+7,2n+4] & \text{if $k$ is odd} \\
[\{2n+7,2n+3\}^{\lfloor (k-2)/2\rfloor},2n+6] & \text{if $k$ is even} 
    \end{cases}
\end{equation}


\begin{lemma} \label{lem:matrix} Define \[ A_n:= \begin{pmatrix} 2 & 1 \\ 1 & 0 \end{pmatrix}^2 \begin{pmatrix} 2n+3 & 1 \\ 1& 0 \end{pmatrix} 
=\begin{pmatrix} 17+10n & 5 \\ 7+4n & 2 \end{pmatrix}\] 
Then the
 following identity holds:
  \[ A_n\begin{pmatrix} \ov{p}_k(n) \\ \ov{q}_k(n) \end{pmatrix}=\begin{pmatrix} p_k(n) \\ q_k(n) \end{pmatrix}\]
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
This identity holds when $k=0,1$ and hence holds for all $k$ since  the quantities $p_k(n),q_k(n), \ov{p}_k(n), \ov{q}_k(n)$
evolve by the same linear recursion.
\end{proof}

\begin{cor}  \label{cor:CFEnk}
    The continued fraction expansion of the centers of $\bE_k(n)$ are as follows:
    \begin{align*} p_0(n)/q_0(n)=[2], \quad p_1(n)/q_1(n)=[2;2,2n+4], 
    \end{align*}
    and for $k \geq 2:$
    \[  p_k(n)/q_k(n)=\begin{cases} 
    [2;2,2n+3,\{2n+7,2n+3\}^{\lfloor(k-2)/2\rfloor},2n+6] & \text{if $k$ is even} \\
   [2;2,2n+3,\{2n+7,2n+3\}^{\lfloor (k-2)/2\rfloor},2n+7,2n+4] & \text{if $k$ is odd} \\
    \end{cases}
    \]
\end{cor}
\begin{proof}
    This can be seen from computing the continued fractions of $p_k/q_k$ for small $k$ and then 
using the fact that $A_n$ is the composite of the three  linear transformations that take  the fraction $p'/q': = [\ell_0;\ell_1,\dots\ell_k]$
(thought of as a vector  $(p',q')$) to the fraction $p/q = [2;2,2n+3,\ell_0,\ell_1,\dots\ell_k]$.
\end{proof}




The next lemma collects some identities about $\bB_k(n)$ and $\bE_k(n).$ Here, we drop the $k$ and $n$ subscript assuming they are constant. 
\begin{lemma} \label{lem:cremonaIdent}
    The classes $\bB_k(n)$ satisfy the following identities:
      \begin{align*} 
      (2n+5)\ov{d}&=(2+n)\ov{p}+(3+n)\ov{q} \\
      (2n+5)\ov{m}&=(1+n)\ov{p}+(4+n)\ov{q}.
  \end{align*}
     The classes $\bE_k(n)$ satisfy the following identities
    \[ \btm=(d-q,q,(d-2q)^{\times 2},(5q-d-p)^{\times (2n+5)})\] and 
   \[d(5+2n)+2(2+n)p-2(11+5n)q=0.\]
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
    The identities can be checked for $\bB_k(n)$ and $\bE_k(n)$ with $k=0,1.$ As the identities are linear, they follow by induction since $\bB_k$ and $\bE_k$ are defined recursively. 
\end{proof}


Here we show that $\bE_k(n)$ is Cremona equivalent to $\bB_k(n)$. To conclude that $\bE_k(n)$ is perfect, we use the following result from \cite{BHM}:
\begin{lemma}{\cite[Proposition 79]{BHM}} \label{lem:bBperfect}
    The classes $\bB_{k}(n)$ are exceptional classes. 
\end{lemma}

\begin{lemma} \label{lem:classesPerfect}
    For $k \geq 2$, the class $\bE_k(n)$ reduces to the class $\bB_k(n)$. In particular, the classes $\bE_k(n)$ are perfect. 
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
In this proof, we continually rearrange the classes $\bE,$ so we will only use a semicolon to distinguish the first element of $\bE$. Further, we will simplify notation by omitting the decorations of $n$ and $k$. 
By Corollary~\ref{cor:CFEnk} and \eqref{eq:CFBnk} regarding the continued fraction of $p/q$ and $\ov{p}/\ov{q},$ we have that, 
\[ \bE=(d;\btm,q^{\times 2},p-2q^{\times 2},5q-2p^{\times (2n+3)}; W(\ov{p},\ov{q})).
\]
We aim to reduce $\bE$ to the class
\[ (\ov{d};\ov{{m}};W(\ov{p},\ov{q})).\]
It suffices to show that $(d;\btm,q^{\times 2},(p-2q)^{\times 2},(5q-2p)^{\times (2n+3)})$ can be reduced to $(\ov{d};\ov{{m}}).$

By Lemma~\ref{lem:cremonaIdent}, we can rearrange this to
\[ v_0:=(d;d-q,q^{\times 3},(d-2q)^{\times 2},(p-2q)^{\times 2},(5q-2p)^{\times (2n+3)},(5q-d-p)^{\times (2n+5)}).\]

We compute that 
\[ c_{4,6,7}c_{1,4,5}c_{1,2,3}v=\bigl(-p+3q;0^{\times 3},5q-d-p,0,d-p,0,p-2q,(5q-2p)^{\times (2n+3)},(5q-d-p)^{\times (2n+5)}\bigr).\]
Rearranging this and dropping the zeros, gives that $v_0$ is Cremona equivalent to
\[ v_1:=\bigl(3p-q;p-2q,(5q-2p)^{\times (2n+3)},(5q-d-p)^{\times (2n+6)},d-p\bigr).\]
We now take a composition of $n+1$ Cremona moves 
\[c_{1,2n+2,2n+3} \hdots c_{1,4,5}c_{1,2,3}v_1\]
each of these will have defect $(3p-q)-(p-2q)-2(5q-2p)=2p-5q.$ The result of applying these $n+1$ moves to $v_1$ is:
\begin{align*} \bigl(3p-q+(n+1)(2p-5q);&p-2q+(n+1)(2p-5q), \\ &0^{\times (2n+2)},(5q-2p)^{\times (2n+3)},(5q-d-p)^{\times (2n+6)},d-p\bigr)\end{align*}
Simplifying and rearranging, we get that $v_1$ is Cremona equivalent to
\[v_2:=\bigl((1+2n)p-(2+5n)q;(3+2n)p-(7+5n)q,5q-2p,(5q-d-p)^{\times (2n+6)},d-p\bigr).\]
We now apply $c_{1,2,3}v_2$ and rearrange/drop the zeros to get
\[ v_3:=\bigl(d+2(1+n)p-(7+5n)q;d+2(2+n)p-(12+5n)q,(5q-d-p)^{\times (2n+5)},(d-p)^{\times 2}\bigr).\]
We next take a composition of $n+1$ Cremona moves $c_{1,2n+2,2n+3},\hdots c_{1,2,3}$, which will each have a defect of $2d-5q$ resulting in: 
\begin{align*}\bigl(d(3+2n)+(2+2n)p-2q(6+5n);&d(3+2n)+2(2+n)p-(17+10n)q, \\ &(d-p)^{\times 2n+2},(5q-d-p)^{\times 3},(d-p)^{\times 2}\bigr)\end{align*}
We can now take $c_{2n+6,2n+5,2n+4}$ and using Lemma~\ref{lem:cremonaIdent} to simplify and rearrange, we get  this is Cremona equivalent to 
\[ v_4:=\bigl(2d(2+n)+p(3+2n)-(17+10n)q;\
d(3+2n)+2(2+n)p-(17+10n)q, 
(d-p)^{\times (2n+4)},0^{\times 3}\bigr).
\]
We then take the composition of the $n+2$ Cremona moves $c_{1,2n+4,2n+5}\hdots c_{1,2,3}$, which will each have a defect of $d-p$ to get 
\[ \bigl(d(2+n)+(5+3n)p-(17+10n)q; d(1+n)+3(2+n)p-(17+10n)q\bigr).\]
Using Lemma~\ref{lem:cremonaIdent}~(ii), this simplifies to:
\[ v_5:=\bigr(-d(3+n)+(1+n)p+5q; -d(4+n)+(2+n)p+5q\bigl).\]
The claim is that
\[ \ov{d}=-d(3+n)+(1+n)p+5q \quad \text{and} \quad \ov{m}=-d(4+n)+(2+n)p+5q.\]

By Lemma~\ref{lem:matrix}, we get 
$(17+10n)\ov{p}+5\ov{q}=p$ and $(7+4n)\ov{p}+2\ov{q}=q.$ Hence writing $v_5$ in terms of $\ov{p}$ and $\ov{q}$ (first using Lemma~\ref{lem:cremonaIdent} to write $d$ in terms of $p,q$), we get that 
\[ v_5=\left(\frac{(2+n)\ov{p}+(3+n)\ov{q}}{5+2n};\frac{(1+n)\ov{p}+(4+n)\ov{q}}{5+2n}\right)=(\ov{d},\ov{m})\]
where the last equality follows from Lemma~\ref{lem:cremonaIdent}.
\end{proof}


\subsection{Classes are obstructive}

We now check that condition (i) in Proposition~\ref{prop:stair} holds.
In view of Lemma~\ref{lem:unobs} we know that each step $\bE_n$ is live at its center for $\Om_B$ where 
 $B=(1;\frac{m_1}{d},\hdots,\frac{m_n}{d}),$ and need to check that it remains obstructive for the limiting domain.


\begin{lemma} \label{lem:nontrivial}
    For $k,n \geq 0$, the classes $\bE_k(n)$ are obstructive at their centers $p_k(n)/q_k(n)$ for $c_{\bm{b}_n}.$
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
We must show that
\begin{equation} \label{eq:nontrivEx}
     \frac{p_k}{d_k-\Tm \cdot \bm{b}_n} > \sqrt{\frac{p_k}{q_k \cdot \Vol}}
\end{equation}
where we have suppressed the $n$ for ease of notation and $\Vol:=\Vol(\Omega_n)$. 
Using \eqref{eqn:bbn} and Lemma~\ref{lem:cremonaIdent}, we have 
\begin{align*} \btm \cdot \bm{b}&=\left(d_k-q_k,q_k,d_k-2q_k^{\times 2},5q_k-d_k-p_k^{\times 2n+5}\right) \cdot \bm{b}  \\
&=\frac{d_k}{2}+\be_n(d_k(5+3n)-(5+2n)p_k+5q_k).
\end{align*}
Hence, \eqref{eq:nontrivEx} is equivalent to
\[ \Vol>\frac{(d_k-(\frac{d_k}{2}+\be(d_k(5+3n)-(5+2n)p_k+5q_k)))^2}{p_kq_k}.\]
Further, by Lemma~\ref{lem:cremonaIdent}, we can write $d_k$ in terms of $p_k,q_k$ to get that the class is nontrivial if 
\begin{align} \label{eq:obsIdent}
     \Vol&>\frac{(-(2+n)p_k+\be(10n^2+42n+45)(p_k-3q_k)+(11+5n)q_k)^2}{(5+2n)^2p_kq_k} \\ \notag
     &=\frac{1}{(5+2n)^2}\left(\sqrt\frac{p_k}{q_k}(-(2+n)+1/\be(1/2-\Vol))+\sqrt\frac{q_k}{p_k}((11+5n)-3/\be(1/2-\Vol))\right)^2,
\end{align}
where we simplified the right hand side 
using  $\be_n^2(10n^2+42n+45)=1/2-\vol$ from  \eqref{eq:ident}.
To show \eqref{eq:obsIdent} holds, we show that for a fixed $n,$ the right hand side of \eqref{eq:obsIdent} strictly increases in $k$ 
with limit equal to 
$\Vol.$

We first show that the right hand side increases. Define $g_1(n)$ and $g_2(n)$ as follows \[ (5+2n)g_1(n)=-(2+n)+1/\be(1/2-\vol) \quad \text{and} \quad  (5+2n)g_2(n)=(11+5n)-3/\be(1/2-\vol).\] 
Note, that $g_2(n)+3g_1(n)=1.$ 
As $p_k/q_k$ decreases as $k$ increases and $2 \leq p_k/q_k \leq 3,$ to show that the right hand side of \eqref{eq:obsIdent} increases, we show that 
\[f(z)=g_1(n)\sqrt{z}+(1-3g_1(n))\sqrt{1/z} \]
decreases for $z \leq 3.$

We have that $2f'(z)=\frac{g_1(n)}{\sqrt{z}}-\frac{1-3g_1(n)}{z^{3/2}}$, so $f'(z) <0$ if
\[   z  \leq \frac{1}{g_1(n)}-3. \]
As $z \leq 3,$ we need to verify that $g_1(n) \leq \frac{1}{6}.$ The function $0 \leq g_1(n) \leq g_1(0)\approx 0.018$. We conclude that the right hand side of \eqref{eq:obsIdent} increases in $k.$

Now, we must show that \eqref{eq:obsIdent} is an equality in the limit as $k \to \infty.$

Writing $p_k=P\la^k+\ov{P}\ov{\la}^k$ and $q_k=Q\la^k+\ov{Q}\ov{\la}^k$ as in Lemma~\ref{lem:recur}, we have that limit of the right hand side is given by 


\[\frac{(P^2g_1(n)^2+Q^2g_2(n)^2+2g_1(n)g_2(n)PQ)}{(5+2n)^2PQ} =\frac{1}{(5+2n)^2}(z_\infty g_1(n)+1/z_\infty g_2(n)+2g_1(n)g_2(n).)\]


We can simplify this to
\[\Vol^2\frac{8(155+\sqrt{\si}(17+8n)+2n(73+17n))}{(13+6n+\sqrt{\si})(29+14n+3\sqrt{\si})} \]
where $\si=(3+2n)(7+2n).$ Thus, we want to show that
\[ \Vol=\Vol^2\frac{8(155+\sqrt{\si}(17+8n)+2n(73+17n)}{(13+6n+\sqrt{\si})(29+14n+3\sqrt{\si})},\]
which holds if 
\begin{equation}\label{eq:volObs} \frac{8(155+\sqrt{\si}(17+8n)+2n(73+17n)}{(13+6n+\sqrt{\si})(29+14n+3\sqrt{\si})}=1/\Vol.
\end{equation}
This can be checked as by \eqref{eq:ident}
\begin{align*} \Vol&=\tfrac12-\be_n^2(45+10n^2+42n) \\
&=\frac12+\frac{-45-2n(21+5n)}{(17+8n+\sqrt{\si})^2} \\
&=\frac{110+(17+8n)\sqrt{\si}+8n(13+3n)}{310+(34+16n)\sqrt{\si}+4n(73+17n)},
\end{align*}
which can be checked to be the reciprocal of \eqref{eq:volObs}
\end{proof}



