\subsection{MIRI and NIRCam images and H$\alpha$ line map} \label{subsect:MIRI_NIRCAM_IMAGES_MAP}

Previous HST images showed that B14-65666 is composed of two spatially separated galaxies in the rest-frame UV, a brighter galaxy in the northeast (galaxy E) and a fainter galaxy in the southwest (galaxy W) (\citealt{bowler_2017_unveiling}). ALMA data further demonstrated that the [O\,III]\,88\,$\mu$m and [C\,II]\,158\,$\mu$m emission lines can be spatially and spectrally decomposed into two components associated with galaxies E and W (\citealt{Hashimoto+18}). NIRCam images revealed the complex morphology of the individual galaxy components (\citealt{sugahara_2024_rioja}): galaxy E has a compact core surrounded by diffuse extended optical emission, while galaxy W displays elongated clumpy morphology. 

MIRIM imaging and the MRS cover the emission line of H$\alpha$ together with the continuum at 0.7\,$\mu$m. MIRI observations show a similar trend as NIRCam images. MRS shows an extended H$\alpha$ emission associated with E and W galaxy components, additionally, each of these galaxy components is spectroscopically resolved as already presented in ALMA  observations (\citealt{hashimoto_2019_big}). We present MIRI and NIRCam images in Fig. \ref{fig:all}.



For this study, we define a total elliptical aperture for the whole system (named `galaxies E+W') with semi-major axis of 0.456\," and semi-minor axis of 0.343\,", and position angle (PA) of 85.83º.  
In the case of the galaxy E we define an elliptical aperture with semi-major axis of 0.211\," and semi-minor axis of 0.201\,", position angle (PA) of 112.83º. 
For the galaxy W we extract an ellipse with 0.228\," and 0.177\," semi-major and semi-minor axis respectively, PA of 112.83º. The coordinates of the center are shown in Table \ref{tab:results}. 
The E and W elliptical apertures are compatible with the main emission of these galaxy components in our MIRIM F560W image. `Galaxies E+W' elliptical aperture covers the extended H$\alpha$ emission from the MRS H$\alpha$ line map. The H$\alpha$ line map is generated by integrating the H$\alpha$ line emission, centered at the H$\alpha$ peak of galaxies E+W (5.3510\,$\mu$m) in the velocity range -500\,<\,{\it v}\,[km\,s$^{-1}$]\,<\,300. See Fig. \ref{fig:all}.




