\subsection{Mass-size relation} \label{subSect:mass-size} 

The two galaxies of the B14-65666 system occupy a very different location in the stellar mass-size plane (see Fig. \ref{fig:mass-size}). 
Galaxy W with an effective radius of 348 pc and a stellar mass of (8\,$\pm$\,4) $\times$ 10$^8$ M$_{\odot}$, is consistent with the expected values according to the relations derived for high redshift galaxies from the JADES program (redshifts 4 to 10; \citealt{langeroodi_2023}), and from the CEERS, PRIMER-UDS, and PRIMER-COSMOS surveys (redshifts 3 to 9; \citealt{allen2024galaxysizemassbuildup}). Therefore, galaxy W appears as an average galaxy at redshift of about 7. On the other hand, the second component of the system, galaxy E, appears as deviating from the standard relations, showing a very small, compact size (upper limit of 63 pc to the effective radius) for a stellar mass of 15 ($\pm$\,9)\,$\times$\,10$^8$\,M$_{\odot}$. This translates into a high stellar mass surface density (6\,$\times$\,$10^{4}$\,M$_{\odot}$\,pc$^{-2}$ for E, versus 1\,$\times\,10^{3}$ M$_{\odot}$\,pc$^{-2}$ for W), close to the limit of the highest values ($10^{5.5}$\,M$_{\odot}$\,pc$^{-2}$) measured in the stellar clusters and nuclei of low-{\it z} galaxies (\citealt{grudic_19}). Galaxies as compact as galaxy E and with similar stellar surface densities are not uncommon at high-$z$ (\citealt{alvarez25}). Star-forming clumps and galaxies with effective radius in the 30 to 200 pc range and a wide range of mass (4\,$\times$\,10$^7$ to 2\,$\times$\,10$^{10}$ M$_{\odot}$) are already known at redshifts above 6 (see Fig. \ref{fig:mass-size}; \citealt{Claeyssens_2023}; \citealt{baggen23}).

Thus, B14-65666 is identified as a system that consists of a pair of interacting galaxies, with  similar stellar masses (within a factor of 2) but with one of the galaxies (E) being extremely compact and already showing stellar mass surface densities close to those measured in the nuclei of galaxies at low redshift. Even more, the galaxies of the B14-65666 system are much less massive ($\approx$\,$\times$\,20\,--\,30), and far more compact ($\approx$\,$\times$\,10\,--\,60) than the main-sequence and the most luminous star-forming galaxies at low redshift, with effective radius $\approx$\,3.5 kpc (\citealt{leroy21}), and $\approx$\,2.2 kpc (\citealt{bellocchi_2022}) respectively (see Fig. \ref{fig:mass-size}). Thus, the galaxies E and W appear as building up their stellar mass as they go through the advanced phases of the interaction/merging process. 



