is an isomorphism $\varphi:\mathbb{k}[X] \longrightarrow\mathbb{k}[Y]$ of underlying $\mathbb{k}$-vector spaces. As in the preceding example, it only suffices to check that $\phi(e_{x_6}  e_{x_i})=\phi(e_{x_6})  \phi(e_{x_i})$ and $\phi(e_{x_i}  e_{x_6})=\phi(e_{x_i})  \phi(e_{x_6})$  for all $i$. These can be verified by straightforward computation.  For example,  $\phi(e_{x_1}e_{x_6})=\phi(e_{x_2})=e_{y_2}=e_{y_1}(e_{y_5}+e_{y_6}-e_{y_7})=\phi(e_{x_1})  \phi(e_{x_6})$ and $\phi(e_{x_6}e_{x_6})= e_{y_5}+e_{y_6}-e_{y_7}=(e_{y_5}+e_{y_6}-e_{y_7})(e_{y_5}+e_{y_6}-e_{y_7}) =\phi(e_{x_6})  \phi(e_{x_6}).$  
}
