  % after \\: \hline or \cline{col1-col2} \cline{col3-col4} ...
$*$ & 1 & 2 & 3 & 4 &5 &6 \\
  \hline \hline
1 & 1 & 1 & 5 & 6 & 3 & 4 \\
\hline
2 & 2 & 2 & 6 & 5 & 4 & 3 \\
\hline
3 & 5 & 6 & 3  & 3 & 1 & 2\\
\hline
4 & 6 & 5 & 4 & 4 & 2 & 1 \\
\hline
5 & 3 & 4 & 1  & 2 & 5 & 5\\
\hline
6 & 4 & 3 & 2  & 1 & 6 & 6\\
  \hline
\end{tabular}.
$$
Note that $X$ has trivial subquandles $\{1,2\}$, $\{3, 4\}$ and $\{5, 6\}$. Hence, by Proposition \ref{trivial subquandle idempotent}, the elements
$\alpha e_1+(1-\alpha) e_2$, $\beta e_3 +(1-\beta)e_4$ and $\gamma e_5+(1-\gamma)e_6$  are non-trivial idempotents of $\mathbb{Z}[X]$ for each $\alpha, \beta, \gamma \in \mathbb{Z}$.}
\end{example}

