\begin{example} \label{extex} {\rm  
 Let $\mathbb{k}$ be a ring in which $2$ is invertible. Let $X=\mathbb{k}^2$ and $\displaystyle r= \left[ \begin{array}{cc} I-Y & Y \\ Z & I-Z \end{array} \right]$, where $\displaystyle Y=\left[ \begin{array}{cc} 1 & s \\ 0 & 1 \end{array} \right]$, $\displaystyle Z=\left[ \begin{array}{cc} 1 & t \\ 0 & 1 \end{array} \right]$ and $s, t \in \mathbb{k}$. Then, by Example~\ref{YBEexamples}, $(X, r)$ is a solution to the Yang--Baxter equation. Let $A=X$ and $V=A \times X$.  Let $\psi : X \times X \rightarrow A$ be defined by 
