We used the Next Generation Palomar Spectrograph (NGPS) instrument on the Palomar 200-inch telescope to observe \ti on 2025 July 03 07:27:50 under program 2025A$\_$P23 (PI: M. Graham). At the time of the Palomar 200-inch observations, \ti was located at a right ascension and declination of 18:03:06.13 and -18:40:05.9, and was located 4.43 au from the Sun, 3.43 au from the Earth, had a phase angle of 2.6 degrees. NGPS possesses a guide camera with a 4 arcmin x 4 arcmin field of view, 0.26 arcsec/pixel spatial scale \citep[][]{Tang2023,Kasliwal2024}, and SDSS-equivalent g, r, and i filters \citep[$\mathrm{\lambda_{eff}}$ = 467.2 nm/614.1 nm/745.8 nm, FWHM = 126.3 nm/115.0 nm/68.3 nm;][]{Gunn1998}. The comet was tracked at its sky motion rate of 75.77 arcsec/h. Two g-band exposures and two i-band exposures were taken with a total cumulative exposure time of 150 s, and three r-band exposures were taken with a total cumulative exposure time of 270 s. Additionally, a sidereal-tracked image containing \ti was taken to provide star detections to compare with the surface brightness profile of \tins. The seeing in r-band was $\sim$1.6 arcsec measured by determining the full-width at half-maximum of background stars, and the g, r, and i images were taken over an airmass range of 1.63 to 2.07. Spectra were also taken by NGPS, which will be described in a forthcoming paper by Belyakov et al.. A Table containing details of the observations is presented in Table~S1 of the Supplemental Material.

The NGPS images were reduced using biases and flats taken for each of the filters, and were stacked as seen in the mosaic of the g, r, and i images in panels A-C of Figure \ref{figprof}. The g, r, and i-band data were calibrated using the Pan-STARRS1 catalog \citep[][]{Chambers2016} and the colour transformations from \citep[][]{Tonry2012} to convert Pan-STARR1 photometry magnitudes into SDSS photometry magnitudes. The g, r, and i-band photometry of \ti was measured using a 4\arcsec~radius circular aperture (10,000 km from the comet's nucleus at its 3.43 au distance from the Earth) and then subtracting from it the median pixel value within a 6.5--7.8\arcsec~(25--30 pixels) radius sky background annulus.

