\subsection{Visible photometry}

We computed the brightness of \ti using 4 arcsec apertures (10,000 km from the nucleus at the 3.43 au distance of the comet on 2024 July 3) with g = 18.27 $\pm$ 0.02, r = 17.83 $\pm$ 0.01, and i = 17.68 $\pm$ 0.02 in the NGPS images. We used the r-band stack to search for evidence of an extended coma in \tins. The surface brightness profile extracted from the r-band images of \ti (blue line in Panel D of Figure \ref{figprof}) shows evidence of being extended, having $>$2-10 times the flux compared to the surface brightness profile of a comparison star (orange line in the same panel) outside of 1.3 arcsec of both profiles. The FWHM of the coma in the r-band stack is $\sim$2.2 arcsec compared to the 1.6 arcsec FWHM of background stars in the sidereal-tracked images. \ti's colours are g-r = 0.43$\pm$0.02, r-i = 0.16$\pm$0.02, g-i = 0.59$\pm$0.03, corresponding to a spectral slope of 1.3$\pm$0.9 $\%$/100 nm, significantly bluer than interstellar comet 2I/Borisov which had g-r = 0.63$\pm$0.05, r-i = 0.20$\pm$0.02, g-i = 0.83$\pm$0.05. We plot the g-r and r-i colours of \ti in Figure \ref{figcolor}.

