Moreover, the $do$-operator forces variables to take certain values, regardless of the original causal mechanism. Graphically, $do(X=x)$ means deleting edges incoming to $X$ and setting $X=x$. Probabilities involving $do(x)$ are normal probabilities in submodel $M_x$: $P(Y=y \mid \text{do}(X=x)) = P_{M_x} (y)$.




\noindent\textbf{Counterfactual inference} \label{Section: two approaches to counterfactual inference}
The latent distribution $P(U)$ allows one to define probabilities of counterfactual queries, $P(Y_{y}=y) = \sum_{u \mid Y_{x}(u)=y} P(u).$ For $x \neq x'$ one can also define joint counterfactual probabilities,
$P(Y_{x}=y, Y_{x'}=y') = \sum_{u \mid Y_{x}(u)=y,\text{ }\& Y_{x'}(u)=y'} P(u).$
Moreover, one can define a counterfactual distribution given seemingly contradictory evidence. Given a set of observed evidence variables $E$, consider the probability $ P(Y_{{x}}={y}' \mid E = {e})$.

\begin{define}[Counterfactual]\label{def:counterfactual}
% \noindent\textbf{Definition 2(Counterfactual):}\emph{
The counterfactual sentence ``$Y$ would be $y$ (in situation $U=u$), had $X$ been $x$'', denoted $Y_{x}(u) = y$, corresponds to $Y=y$ in submodel $M_x$ for $U = u$.
% }
\end{define}

%, which reads ``what is the probability that $Y$ would have been ${y}'$, given that $E$ was observed to be ${e}$, if $X$ had been ${x}$?''
