\subsection*{Exposure}

Since tweets were gathered at, or shortly after, the time of posting, we have no indicators of exposure, such as the number of retweets and likes.
We therefore rely on an indirect indicator of exposure, namely the number of followers of an account at the time of posting. More formally, we quantify the exposure as the number of followers $K_u(m)$ of the user $u$ who posted message $m$ at time $t$. The total exposure for a set of tweets is defined as the sum of the number of followers $K_u$. That is, let $S$ be a set of tweets (e.g. reliable tweets, unreliable tweets, tweets in 2020, all tweets), then the exposure $E$ of $S$ is defined as
\begin{equation}
    E(S) = \sum_{m \in S} K_u(m).
\end{equation}

