\subsection{Jump coefficients \texorpdfstring{$D_1$, $D_2$, and $D_4$}{D1, D2, and D4}}

Fig.~\ref{km_coefficients_plot} shows the estimated jump coefficients \( D_1(r) \), \( D_2(r) \), and \( D_4(r) \) plotted against the return variable \( r \) for the three countries. Across all cases, the drift coefficient \( D_1(r) \) exhibits a strong linear dependence, consistent with mean-reverting behavior in the exchange rate dynamics. The diffusion coefficient \( D_2(r) \) demonstrates a clear nonlinear dependence on \( r \), captured by a second-order polynomial fit, suggesting state-dependent volatility. This observation is consistent with findings from previous studies that report non-constant and return-dependent volatility in financial time series (see\cite{ghasemi2007markov} for instance).

To assess the validity of the Fokker–Planck approximation, we examine the magnitude of \( D_4(r) \) relative to \( D_2(r) \). In all three data sets, \( D_4(r) \) remains negligible compared to \( D_2(r) \), satisfying the conditions of the Pawula's theorem \cite{pawula1967approximation} and thereby justifying the truncation of the Kramers–Moyal expansion at second order. This supports the use of a Langevin-type stochastic model with state-dependent drift and diffusion terms \cite{Tabar2019}.

