\begin{abstract}
\noindent
Following Cornelissen, Li, Marcolli, and Smit, this short paper proves that the isomorphism of the Deligne-Ribet monoids $DR_K, DR_L$ for two number fields $K, L$ implies the field isomorphism $K \simeq L$. Thus the Deligne-Ribet monoid $DR_K$ gives a complete invariant of the number field $K$ as in the case of the absolute Galois group $G_K$. 
\end{abstract}
%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%
\section{Introduction}
\label{s1}
Cornelissen, Li, Marcolli, and Smit \cite{Cornelissen_Li_Marcolli_Smit} proved that two number fields $K, L$ are isomorphic as fields if and only if there exists a topological-monoid isomorphism $\phi: DR_K \rightarrow DR_L$ of their Deligne-Ribet monoids \emph{in such a way that $\phi$ restricts to the monoid isomorphism $\phi: I_K \rightarrow I_L$ of the submonoids $I_K \subseteq DR_K$, $I_L \subseteq DR_L$ of the non-zero integral ideals of $K, L$}. The current paper then proves that this latter italic assumption can be actually removed; and thus we conclude:

\begin{thm}
\label{completeness of DR_K}
Two number fields $K, L$ are isomorphic as fields if and only if their Deligne-Ribet monoids $DR_K, DR_L$ are isomorphic as topological monoids.
\end{thm}

Our proof is based on some elementary ideas from the theory of profinite semigroups, which will highlight how the semigroup structure of $DR_K$ is useful to reconstruct the arithmetic structure of the number field $K$. A key step of our proof is to classify the idempotents of $DR_K$ in terms of sets of the maximal ideals $\idp \in P_K$; we will see that certain idempotents $e_S$ of $DR_K$, which are explicitly constructed from $S \subseteq P_K$ and played a key role in \cite{Cornelissen_Li_Marcolli_Smit} too, actually \emph{exhaust} all idempotents of $DR_K$. That is:

\begin{prop}
Let $S \subseteq P_K$ be any subset of the set $P_K$ of maximal ideals of the integer ring $O_K$ of $K$; and define an idempotent $e_S \in DR_K$ as follows (cf.\ \S 7 \cite{Cornelissen_Li_Marcolli_Smit}, or \S \ref{s2} below): 
\begin{eqnarray}
 e_S &:=& [1_S, 1],
\end{eqnarray}
Then every idempotent $e \in DR_K$ is equal to $e_{S_e}$ for some unique subset $S_e \subseteq P_K$, which is explicitly given as follows: writing $e = [\rho_e, s_e] \in \hat{O}_K \times_{\hat{O}_K^\times} G_K^{ab} \simeq DR_K$, 
\begin{eqnarray}
 S_e &:=& \bigl \{ \idp \in P_K \mid v_\idp(\rho_{e, \idp}) = 0 \bigr\}.
\end{eqnarray}
\end{prop}

In other words, we have a bijective correspondence between the set $\ee_K$ of idempotents of $DR_K$ and the set $\pp(P_K)$ of the subsets of $P_K$. We also note that a natural poset-structure on idempotents of $DR_K$ can be defined by $e \leq e'$ if and only if $e \cdot e' = e$, which is in fact equivalent to the inclusion $S_e \subseteq S_{e'}$. Therefore, the above correspondence $\ee_K \rightarrow \pp(P_K)$ is actually an isomorphism of posets; in particular, by the poset structure, it makes sense to define an idempotent $e \in \ee_K$ to be \emph{maximal} by saying that (i) $e\neq 1$ and (ii) $e \leq e'$ implies that $e'=e$ or $e'=1$. 

With this concept, we can classify the maximal ideals $\idp \in P_K$ of $O_K$ in the following form:

\begin{cor}
There exists a bijective correspondence between the maximal ideals $\idp \in P_K$ of $O_K$ and the maximal idempotents of $DR_K$. 
\end{cor}

Since the monoid $I_K$ is generated by the maximal ideals $\idp \in P_K$, this correspondence implies that a topological-monoid isomorphism $\phi: DR_K \rightarrow DR_L$ canonically produces a monoid isomorphism $\bar{\phi}: I_K \rightarrow I_L$. This isomorphism $\bar{\phi}: I_K \rightarrow I_L$ itself may not be a restriction of $\phi$, but we prove that $\bar{\phi}: I_K \rightarrow I_L$ extends to a topological-monoid isomorphism $DR_K \rightarrow DR_L$, which does restrict to $\bar{\phi}$; for this extension we shall utilize Theorem 3.1 (ii), of \cite{Cornelissen_deSmit_Li_Marcolli_Smit} due to Cornelissen, de Smit, Li, Marcolli, and Smit. 

