\subsection{Non-termination}
These examples underlined that the operator $\Lt_n\ket{\psi}$ returns the state that is the superposition of all executions starting from $\ket{\psi}$ and terminating in less than $n$ steps. 

Thus the norm $\|\lnSem{\whle{q}{s}}\ket{\psi}\|$ gives a measure of the portion of the state that diverges. Specifically, if the loop diverges, the semantics will be equal to the zero operator (as in \autoref{eq: esLni3}). 
If both terminating and non-terminating executions are in a superposition, the linear semantics will return a state with a norm less than one, and the missing norm value provides a measure of non-termination.
For instance, in \autoref{eq: esLni2} we see that for all $n$, $\|\Lt(q,H_q)\ket{\psi}\| < 1$, and also the norm of the limit, applied to $\ket{\psi}$ will be less than one.
On the other hand, if the program terminates fully, the semantics are defined for all inputs, and the norm $\|\lnSem{\whle{q}{s}}\ket{\psi}\|$ will be equal to 1 (as we see in \autoref{eq: esLni1}).
In general, the norm $\|\lnSem{\whle{q}{s}}\|$ will be equal to 1 if there exists at least one input for which the loop terminates while $\min_{\|\ket{\psi}\| = 1} \|\lnSem{\whle{q}{s}}\ket{\psi}\| = 1$ if and only if the loop terminates for all inputs.
