It can be seen that each recursive call consumes a copy of the guard variable, and to achieve infinite recursion, we need an infinite number of copies of the same guard variables. 
For defining their semantics, Ying et al. employ a formalism from quantum physics related to multi-particle systems, specifically Fock spaces~\cite{fock} and second quantization~\cite{bosonfermion}. 
In particular, they consider free Fock spaces, i.e., Hilbert spaces that describe quantum states with a variable number of indistinguishable particles, constructed as $\oplus_{n=1}^\infty \Hilb^{\otimes n}$, i.e., the direct sum of tensor powers of the single-particle Hilbert space $\Hilb$.
