\cite{choquet2011cauchy} offers the most detailed treatment of the Cauchy problem for the Einstein equations with data on a future null cone. For the analysis, \cite{choquet2011cauchy} introduces a background (target) metric $\hat{g}$ to characterize the gauge freedom in a tensorial way. Letting $\hat{D}$ and $\hat{\Gamma}^\lambda_{\alpha\beta}$ be the covariant derivative and the Chrystoffel symbols of $\hat{g}$ (we put hats to expressions depending on $\hat{g}$), the Ricci tensor of the physical metric, $g$, can be written as
\begin{equation}
	R_{\alpha\beta}=R_{\alpha\beta}^{(\hat{H})}+\frac{1}{2}(g_{\alpha\lambda}\hat{D}_\beta \hat{H}^\lambda+g_{\beta\lambda}\hat{D}_\alpha \hat{H}^\lambda)
\end{equation} 
with
\begin{equation}
	\hat{H}^\lambda:=g^{\alpha\beta}(\Gamma^\lambda_{\alpha\beta}-\hat{\Gamma}^\lambda_{\alpha\beta})
\end{equation}
\begin{equation}
	R_{\alpha\beta}^{(\hat{H})}:=-\frac{1}{2}g^{\lambda\mu}\hat{D}_{\lambda}\hat{D}_\mu g_{\alpha\beta}+\hat{f}[g,\hat{D}g]_{\alpha\beta}
\end{equation}
where $\hat{f}[g,\hat{D}g]_{\alpha\beta}$ depends on $g$ and $\hat{g}$, but is independent of second derivatives of $g$. The tensor $R_{\alpha\beta}^{(\hat{H})}$ is called the reduced Ricci tensor in $\hat{g}$-wave-map gauge and is quasilinear and quasidiagonal on $g$.

The first important theorem is \textbf{Theorem 3.1} (The Cagnac-Dossa theorem)\footnote{For details of theorem 3.1 and 3.2 see the next section} which proves the existence and unicity of solutions for the Cauchy problem for hyperbolic, quasilinear, quasidiagonal equations with data on a future null cone, $C_O$, which consists only in the value of the function, $f|_{C_O}$, (and no its derivatives) such that $f|_{C_O}(O)=0$, where $O$ is the vertex of $C_O$.

If we write $g=\eta+h$, with $\eta$ the Minkowski metric, and work in coordinates $\{y^\alpha\}$ such that $\eta=-(dy^0)^2+(dy^1)^2+(dy^2)^2+(dy^3)^2$ around $O$, then, as an application of theorem 3.1, in \textbf{Theorem 3.2} is showed that
the if we prescribed initial data, $\bar{g}=\bar{\eta}+\bar{h}$, such that $\bar{h}_{\alpha\beta}(O)=0$, the ($\hat{g}$-wave-gauge) reduced Einstein equations:
\begin{equation}
	R^{(\hat{H})}_{\alpha\beta}=T_{\alpha\beta}-\frac{1}{2}Tg_{\alpha\beta}:=\rho_{\alpha\beta}
\end{equation}
admit a unique solution in a neighborhood of the vertex. From now on a bar over an object denotes the restriction of that object to $C_O$. 

By (2), if $\hat{H}^\lambda=0$ the above solution also satisfies the full Einstein equations. Therefore, we fix the gauge imposing
\begin{equation}
	\hat{H}^\lambda:=g^{\alpha\beta}(\Gamma^\lambda_{\alpha\beta}-\hat{\Gamma}^\lambda_{\alpha\beta})=0.
\end{equation}
Note that this condition changes for different choices of $\hat{g}$, but is independent of the coordinate system.
The following theorem gives sufficient conditions to guarantee that $\hat{H}^\lambda=0$

\vspace{2mm}

\textit{\textbf{Theorem 3.3}: Let $g^{(\hat{H})}$ a solution of the $\hat{g}$-reduced Einstein equations. Then $g^{(\hat{H})}$ will be a solution of the full Einstein equations if $\overline{\hat{H}}=0$ and $T$ satisfies $\nabla_\alpha T^{\alpha\beta}=0$}

\vspace{2mm} 

So far the analysis has been analogous to the analysis of the standard case where the initial hypersurface is spacelike (the condition $\hat{H}^\lambda=0$ reduces to the condition $\nabla_\alpha\nabla^\alpha x^\lambda=0$ when the target metric is $\eta$, see for example \cite{wald2010general}), but at this point an important difference comes arise, it turns out that in the standard case if the physical constraints
\begin{equation}
