The experiments were conducted on Wulver, an HPC cluster at NJIT \cite{njit_hpc}, using a single core of an AMD EPYC 7753 CPU @ 2.45\,GHz and 512\,GB of RAM.

\subsection{Optimization Approach}


The evaluation demonstrated that while all examined models successfully generated code that integrated seamlessly into the benchmark framework without requiring modifications, DeepSeek R1, Gemini 2.5 Flash, and Grok 3- Think produced implementations that failed to correctly count number of triangles. Consequently, due to these incorrect outputs, the algorithmic details for these three models were excluded from further analysis.

Most models leveraged efficient approaches already present in the benchmark, combining optimized techniques such as degree-based vertex sorting (using the benchmark’s built-in function), hash-based intersection, the Forward Triangle Counting algorithm \cite{schank2005finding,schank2007algorithmic}, and BFS traversal \cite{bader2023triangle}. These strategies, while not groundbreaking, were effectively synthesized into workable solutions. 

Although both Gemini 2.5 Pro and Claude Sonnet 4 Extended employed similar core methods, using the Forward Counting Algorithm combined with sorting and hashing, Claude Sonnet 4 Extended achieved faster performance. This improvement indicates a more effective utilization of memory management and cache locality by the Claude model. Other models achieve their fastest runtimes by leveraging higher peak memory.

ChatGPT o4-mini-high implemented the Forward Triangle Counting Algorithm for its solution. However, its implementation did not incorporate advanced optimizations such as hashing or sorting of nodes to accelerate neighbor intersection computations which led to suboptimal time performance, particularly in graphs with high-degree vertices where efficient lookup mechanisms could reduce redundant comparisons. As a result, its implementation placed third among the LLMs implementations, trailing behind human-written code.


ChatGPT o3 incorporated two human-written helper functions—one for graphs with more than 16,384 edges and another for smaller instances. However, its choice of which function to apply was not optimal compared to other LLMs or the hand-written baseline, leading to a slower runtime despite achieving the lowest peak memory usage compared to other LLMs.

Claude Sonnet 4 also adopted the Forward Triangle Counting Algorithm and incorporated hashing to optimize neighbor intersection operations, a strategy that theoretically should improve performance for common neighbors. However, its implementation exhibited significantly worse time performance compared to other models which shows its poor performance in implementing efficient code.  

The proposed implementations for each model are available in GitHub at https://github.com/AtiehBaratinia/LLMGeneratedCode. Table \ref{t:results1} then summarizes their comparative performance, both runtime and max memory usage, on the RMAT-18 dataset\cite{chakrabarti2004r}. 

To comprehensively evaluate the performance of the generated code, we tested the corrected algorithms on graphs ranging from RMAT-6 to RMAT-18. This allowed us to observe runtime behavior across both small and large graphs. We also included an efficient human-generated implementation of Bader’s algorithm that incorporates sorting vertices, BFS traversal, and hash arrays, as well as two different implementations of Forward Triangle Counting algorithm with hash-based neighbor intersections.

The results in Table \ref{t:results:intel9480} show that Claude 4 Sonnet Extended —and, to a slightly lesser extent, Gemini 2.5 Pro— achieved the fastest runtimes by trading increased peak memory for lower execution time. Both models outperformed every other LLM and even the human‐written baseline, despite relying on existing triangle‐counting techniques rather than inventing new algorithms. This suggests that, while large language models excel at optimizing and refining code, their current capabilities remain focused on improving known methods rather than developing fundamentally new algorithmic advances.

\subsection{Algorithm-Synthesis Appraoch}

In this approach, we assess each model’s capability to produce efficient implementations of established graph algorithms without access to any pre-existing code. Performance is evaluated according to two principal criteria: Ready-To-Use (RTU) Capability and Efficiency Trade-Offs.

\subsubsection{Ready-To-Use (RTU) Capability}
A model is deemed RTU for a given algorithm if it generates code that meets all of the following requirements:
\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Compilability:} The submitted code must compile successfully without any manual modifications.
  \item \textbf{Correctness:} The code must pass our complete suite of predefined test cases.
  \item \textbf{Timeliness:} The implementation must execute within an acceptable runtime threshold, ensuring practical usability.
\end{itemize}
Table \ref{tab:rtu_percentage} reports the RTU rate for each model across the six target graph problems. Results indicate that Claude Sonnet 4 Extended achieves the highest RTU score, successfully generating fully compilable, correct, and timely code for 83\% of the algorithmic tasks. 



