\subsubsection{Efficiency Trade-Offs}

Beyond RTU status, we also evaluate each model’s time–memory trade‐off when executing the generated routines. Specifically, we record two metrics for every model–algorithm pair:
\begin{enumerate}
    \item \textbf{Relative runtime (\(t\))}—normalized so that the fastest implementation for a given algorithm has \(t = 1\).
    \item \textbf{Relative peak memory usage (\(m\))}—normalized so that the implementation using the least memory for that same algorithm has \(m = 1\).
\end{enumerate}

Table \ref{tab:runtime-mem} presents the relative values for each model and its RTU algorithm. Models that did not generate a valid RTU implementation are omitted from the analysis and indicated with a dash.

To compare the models on a single efficiency metric, we introduce a rate that combines relative runtime and peak memory consumption:

\[
\mathrm{rate} \;=\;
\begin{cases}
\dfrac{1}{tm}\,, 
&\parbox[t]{.5\textwidth}{\raggedright if the model produced RTU implementation},\\[1em]
0\,, 
&\text{otherwise}.
\end{cases}
\]

Here, \(t\) is the model’s runtime expressed as a fraction of the fastest implementation, and \(m\) is its maximum memory usage expressed as a fraction of the most memory‑efficient implementation. A higher rate therefore indicates greater overall efficiency. Each model’s overall score is calculated by summing its rates on all six benchmark algorithms; the resulting totals appear in Table \ref{tab:efficiency_rates}.


