\maketitle
\begin{abstract}
We propose a method for multilevel decomposition of generalized entropy (GE) measures that explicitly accounts for nested population structures such as national, regional, and subregional levels.
Standard approaches that estimate GE separately at each level do not guarantee compatibility with multilevel decomposition.
Our method constrains lower-level GE estimates to match higher-level benchmarks while preserving hierarchical relationships across layers.
We apply the method to Japanese income data to estimate GE at the national, prefectural, and municipal levels, decomposing national inequality into between-prefecture and within-prefecture inequality, and further decomposing prefectural GE into between-municipality and within-municipality inequality.

\begin{comment}
We consider the problem of simultaneously estimating the generalized entropy (GE) measure of the population (entire country) and the GE measures of its subpopulations (regions) based on the grouped data and decomposing the GE of the entire country into the between-region and within-region inequality.
When the GE measures are estimated assuming parametric distributions for the country and for each of the regions separately, it is not possible to obtain estimates compatible with the decomposition.
Therefore, to achieve compatibility with the decomposition, we estimate the GE for each region using the constrained Bayes estimator, using the estimate of the GE for the country and that of the between-region inequality as benchmarks.
We apply the proposed method to Japanese data and estimate the GE for the entire country and that of each prefecture, decomposing the GE for the country into between-prefecture inequality and within-prefecture inequality.
Furthermore, we decompose the GE of each prefecture into between-municipality inequality and within-municipality inequality.
\end{comment}

\par\vspace{4mm}
\noindent{\it Keywords}: Benchmarking; Constrained Bayes estimator; Generalized entropy; Income inequality.

